+// Lobium: does this element's box actually carry HOST entropy worth hiding?
+//
+// clientRects fingerprinting reads sub-pixel geometry that varies by machine, and that variation
+// comes from TEXT METRICS - a content-sized box is as wide as the font rasteriser made its glyphs.
+// A box whose width and height are explicit CSS lengths is pure arithmetic: 100px is 100px on every
+// machine, and stays exactly reproducible under a transform. Perturbing it hides nothing and is
+// trivially detectable, because the detector can compute what the value must have been.
+//
+// CreepJS does exactly that, twice (creep.js L4189-4216):
+//   .rect-known  width:100px; height:100px; transform:rotate(45deg)  -> bounding box is 100*sqrt(2)
+//                everywhere, compared against a HARDCODED hash (9d9215cc)
+//   .rect-ghost  width:0; height:0                                   -> every field must read 0
+// Both were flagged purely because we perturbed values that could not have differed between hosts.
+//
+// So: farble content-sized boxes and leave CSS-determined ones exact. This narrows the noise to
+// where the entropy actually is; it does not weaken it.
+static bool LobiumRectCarriesHostEntropy(const Element& element,
+                                         const gfx::RectF& rect) {
+  // A zero-area box says nothing about the host, and noise here is what produced "unknown ghost
+  // dimensions". Checked before the style lookup because it holds however the box was sized.
+  if (rect.width() == 0.0f && rect.height() == 0.0f) {
+    return false;
+  }
+  const ComputedStyle* style = element.GetComputedStyle();
+  if (!style) {
+    return false;
+  }
+  // Both axes fixed => fully specified by CSS. Either axis auto/percentage/content-driven means
+  // layout decided it, and layout is where the machine shows through.
+  return !(style->Width().IsFixed() && style->Height().IsFixed());
+}
